{"version":"ai-mcq-bank-v2-la-batch15-high-quality-rewritten-affected-2026-05-22-stem-fixed-inequality-rendering-fixed-student-facing-qc-2026-05-31-pt-foundations-2026-06-11-pt-batch2-foundations-counting-2026-06-11-pt-batch3-counting-probability-rules-2026-06-11-pt-batch4-conditioning-2026-06-11-pt-batch5-random-variables-2026-06-11-pt-batch6-discrete-distributions-2026-06-11-pt-batch7-distribution-foundations-2026-06-11-pt-batch8-continuous-expectation-2026-06-11-pt-batch9-non-week7-expectation-transform-2026-06-11-pt-batch10-non-week7-process-summary-2026-06-11-pt-batch11-non-week7-final-summary-shape-2026-06-11-quality-round1-2026-06-14-quality-round2-2026-06-14-quality-round3-2026-06-14-quality-round4-deep-2026-06-14+quality-round5-difficulty-2026-06-14+quality-round6-expression-2026-06-14+quality-round7-answer-cue-2026-06-14+quality-round8-answer-shape-2026-06-14+quality-round9-answer-position-2026-06-14+quality-round10-template-padding-2026-06-14+quality-round11-generic-stems-2026-06-14+quality-round12-deterministic-cleanup-2026-06-15+quality-pilot-deep-rework-2026-06-15","concept_id":"Year-1/2a-Math-III-Analysis/metric-and-metric-space.html","concept":{"concept_id":"Year-1/2a-Math-III-Analysis/metric-and-metric-space.html","questions":[{"id":"year_1_2a_math_iii_analysis_metric_and_metric_space_bda40d51_001","difficulty":"basic","question_type":"conceptual","question":"What does a metric space $(\\boldsymbol{X},d)$ consist of?","options":["A set $\\boldsymbol{X}$ and a distance function $d\\colon\\boldsymbol{X}\\times\\boldsymbol{X}\\to[0,+\\infty)$.","Only a set $\\boldsymbol{X}$ with no distance function.","Only a function $d$ with no domain.","A set of matrices with no distance rule."],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"4fcdf193c2bcb5c79081ffec"},{"id":"year_1_2a_math_iii_analysis_metric_and_metric_space_bda40d51_002","difficulty":"basic","question_type":"conceptual","question":"Which property says that $d(\\boldsymbol{x},\\boldsymbol{y}) = 0$ exactly when $\\boldsymbol{x} = \\boldsymbol{y}$?","options":["Symmetry.","Triangle inequality.","Non-negativity only.","Identity of indiscernibles."],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"84c684e9f252ab0d8b2f190f"},{"id":"year_1_2a_math_iii_analysis_metric_and_metric_space_bda40d51_003","difficulty":"basic","question_type":"application","question":"Which value is impossible for a metric distance $d(\\boldsymbol{x},\\boldsymbol{y})$?","options":["$0$","$1$","$-2$","$5$"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"e4e0d974d6e26d972617b7ce"},{"id":"year_1_2a_math_iii_analysis_metric_and_metric_space_bda40d51_004","difficulty":"basic","question_type":"application","question":"If $d(\\boldsymbol{x},\\boldsymbol{y}) = 0$ in a metric space, what must be true?","options":["$\\boldsymbol{x} \\neq \\boldsymbol{y}$","$d(\\boldsymbol{y},\\boldsymbol{x}) = 1$","$\\boldsymbol{X} = \\varnothing$","$\\boldsymbol{x} = \\boldsymbol{y}$"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"2a1332e6a3dff961d2f2c851"},{"id":"year_1_2a_math_iii_analysis_metric_and_metric_space_bda40d51_005","difficulty":"standard","question_type":"conceptual","question":"Which formula expresses symmetry of a metric?","options":["$d(\\boldsymbol{x},\\boldsymbol{y}) = -d(\\boldsymbol{y},\\boldsymbol{x})$","$d(\\boldsymbol{x},\\boldsymbol{y}) = 0$ for all points","$d(\\boldsymbol{x},\\boldsymbol{y}) = d(\\boldsymbol{y},\\boldsymbol{x})$","$d(\\boldsymbol{x},\\boldsymbol{z}) = d(\\boldsymbol{x},\\boldsymbol{y})d(\\boldsymbol{y},\\boldsymbol{z})$"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"24d6a45987ef678eefef4ecc"},{"id":"year_1_2a_math_iii_analysis_metric_and_metric_space_bda40d51_006","difficulty":"standard","question_type":"conceptual","question":"Which formula expresses the triangle inequality?","options":["$d(\\boldsymbol{x},\\boldsymbol{z})\\leq d(\\boldsymbol{x},\\boldsymbol{y})+d(\\boldsymbol{y},\\boldsymbol{z})$","$d(\\boldsymbol{x},\\boldsymbol{z})\\geq d(\\boldsymbol{x},\\boldsymbol{y})+d(\\boldsymbol{y},\\boldsymbol{z})$","$d(\\boldsymbol{x},\\boldsymbol{z}) = d(\\boldsymbol{x},\\boldsymbol{y})-d(\\boldsymbol{y},\\boldsymbol{z})$","$d(\\boldsymbol{x},\\boldsymbol{z}) = 0$ for all $\\boldsymbol{x},\\boldsymbol{z}$"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"b28780d5cc9068abe81b8145"},{"id":"year_1_2a_math_iii_analysis_metric_and_metric_space_bda40d51_007","difficulty":"standard","question_type":"application","question":"Suppose $d(\\boldsymbol{x},\\boldsymbol{y}) = 4$ in a metric space. What is $d(\\boldsymbol{y},\\boldsymbol{x})$?","options":["$4$","$-4$","$0$","$8$"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"33dd9c10c523cf7c03f796c8"},{"id":"year_1_2a_math_iii_analysis_metric_and_metric_space_bda40d51_008","difficulty":"standard","question_type":"application","question":"If $d(\\boldsymbol{x},\\boldsymbol{y}) = 2$ and $d(\\boldsymbol{y},\\boldsymbol{z}) = 3$, what upper bound does the triangle inequality give for $d(\\boldsymbol{x},\\boldsymbol{z})$?","options":["$1$","$5$","$6$","$0$"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"db542da43baaa9e508ef98d1"},{"id":"year_1_2a_math_iii_analysis_metric_and_metric_space_bda40d51_009","difficulty":"challenge","question_type":"conceptual","question":"A proposed distance allows $d(\\boldsymbol{x},\\boldsymbol{y}) = 0$ for two distinct points. Which metric property fails?","options":["Identity of indiscernibles.","Symmetry only.","Triangle inequality only.","Non-negativity only."],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"910d8952b7f926644134efb1"},{"id":"year_1_2a_math_iii_analysis_metric_and_metric_space_bda40d51_010","difficulty":"challenge","question_type":"conceptual","question":"Which proposed distance on $\\mathbb{C}$ satisfies the basic metric requirement that distances are real and non-negative?","options":["$d(z,w)=z-w$","$d(z,w)=\\operatorname{Re}(z-w)$","$d(z,w)=|z-w|$","$d(z,w)=-|z-w|$"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"05645e3731adec4ea396b634"},{"id":"year_1_2a_math_iii_analysis_metric_and_metric_space_bda40d51_011","difficulty":"challenge","question_type":"application","question":"Why is a rule with $d(\\boldsymbol{x},\\boldsymbol{y}) = -1$ for some pair unable to be a metric?","options":["It violates symmetry but not non-negativity.","It violates non-negativity.","It satisfies all metric properties.","It violates only the notation for ordered pairs."],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"3e20e78796a10ec0d6e5877a"},{"id":"year_1_2a_math_iii_analysis_metric_and_metric_space_bda40d51_012","difficulty":"challenge","question_type":"application","question":"For Euclidean distance in $\\mathbb{R}^n$, which formula is given?","options":["$d(\\boldsymbol{x},\\boldsymbol{y}) = \\sum_{i = 1}^{n}(x_i+y_i)$","$d(\\boldsymbol{x},\\boldsymbol{y}) = \\ \\left(\\sum_{i = 1}^{n}(x_i-y_i)^2\\right)^{1/2}$","$d(\\boldsymbol{x},\\boldsymbol{y}) = \\max_{1\\leq i\\leq n}x_i y_i$","$d(\\boldsymbol{x},\\boldsymbol{y}) = 0$ for all $\\boldsymbol{x},\\boldsymbol{y}$"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"7209155d1ad8f11a54b97e04"},{"id":"year_1_2a_math_iii_analysis_metric_and_metric_space_f31c9f44_013","difficulty":"basic","question_type":"conceptual","question":"What is a metric on a set $\\boldsymbol X$?","options":["A function $d:\\boldsymbol X\\times\\boldsymbol X\\to[0,+\\infty)$ satisfying the metric properties.","A subset of $\\boldsymbol X$ only.","A sequence of points in $\\boldsymbol X$.","A scalar field unrelated to distance, for every possible choice of input."],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"9f3de07a42686bac9319c5b0"},{"id":"year_1_2a_math_iii_analysis_metric_and_metric_space_f31c9f44_014","difficulty":"basic","question_type":"conceptual","question":"Which metric property says $d(\\boldsymbol x,\\boldsymbol y)=d(\\boldsymbol y,\\boldsymbol x)$?","options":["Triangle inequality","Non-negativity","Symmetry","Identity of indiscernibles"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"a0d6a6b3c7433fd7bcd73725"},{"id":"year_1_2a_math_iii_analysis_metric_and_metric_space_f31c9f44_015","difficulty":"basic","question_type":"conceptual","question":"Which metric property says $d(\\boldsymbol x,\\boldsymbol y)=0$ exactly when $\\boldsymbol x=\\boldsymbol y$?","options":["Symmetry","Identity of indiscernibles","Triangle inequality","Boundedness"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"e8d45c5f9bcc8e09887a1252"},{"id":"year_1_2a_math_iii_analysis_metric_and_metric_space_f31c9f44_016","difficulty":"basic","question_type":"application","question":"If $d$ is a metric and $\\boldsymbol x\\neq\\boldsymbol y$, which value is impossible for $d(\\boldsymbol x,\\boldsymbol y)$?","options":["$0$","$1$","$2$","$\\frac12$"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"6432f1a4121f40220af4a5ef"},{"id":"year_1_2a_math_iii_analysis_metric_and_metric_space_f31c9f44_017","difficulty":"basic","question_type":"application","question":"If $d(\\boldsymbol x,\\boldsymbol y)=4$ in a metric space, what is $d(\\boldsymbol y,\\boldsymbol x)$?","options":["$-4$","$4$","$0$","It cannot be determined because metrics are not symmetric."],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"6bcc8fe466db2652e5c74f16"},{"id":"year_1_2a_math_iii_analysis_metric_and_metric_space_f31c9f44_018","difficulty":"basic","question_type":"application","question":"Which inequality is the triangle inequality?","options":["$d(\\boldsymbol x,\\boldsymbol y)=d(\\boldsymbol y,\\boldsymbol x)$","$d(\\boldsymbol x,\\boldsymbol y)\\ge0$","$d(\\boldsymbol x,\\boldsymbol x)=1$","$d(\\boldsymbol x,\\boldsymbol z)\\le d(\\boldsymbol x,\\boldsymbol y)+d(\\boldsymbol y,\\boldsymbol z)$"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"19915e7ad4092c6d38f3e652"},{"id":"year_1_2a_math_iii_analysis_metric_and_metric_space_f31c9f44_019","difficulty":"standard","question_type":"conceptual","question":"Why must a metric take values in $[0,+\\infty)$?","options":["Distances must always be integers.","Distances are required to be nonnegative real numbers.","Distances are allowed to be complex numbers.","Distances must be negative when points are distinct."],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"ecee89abc2d5feb50d06ea25"},{"id":"year_1_2a_math_iii_analysis_metric_and_metric_space_f31c9f44_020","difficulty":"standard","question_type":"application","question":"Suppose $d(\\boldsymbol x,\\boldsymbol y)=2$ and $d(\\boldsymbol y,\\boldsymbol z)=3$. What upper bound does the triangle inequality give for $d(\\boldsymbol x,\\boldsymbol z)$?","options":["$1$","$6$","$5$","$0$"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"63dcab511702f96980b32d38"}]}}